The paper is written in a clear and well-organized way; 

the quality of the paper is overall good, except the above raised concerns; 

the originality of the work is relatively fair, limiting to the facts that the tackled scenario is not completely new and the developed method is a simple averaging with least-squares optimization;

 authors have provide the codes, and the reviewer quickly reviewed it without re-running.

content.comment:
When we feed our method a sample $\mathbf{x_0}\mapsto\mathbf{u_0}$ we do not know whether it is an adversary or not, and in the former case, we do not know which strategy between the $k\in\mathcal{K}$ possible it was created with. If that was possible, we would pick the detector $q^{(\bar{k})}$ for the most likely $\bar{k}\in\mathcal{K}$. Instead, all we can do is to interpret

\begin{align}
\mathbb{E}\_{\widehat{Z}\sim q^{(k)}\_{\widehat{Z}|\mathbf{u\_0}}}\left[-\log q\_{\widehat{Z}|\mathbf{u\_0}}\right] = -\sum\_{\widehat{z}\in\\{0, 1\\}} q^{(k)}\_{\widehat{Z}|\mathbf{u\_0}}(\widehat{z}|\mathbf{u\_0})\log q\_{\widehat{Z}|\mathbf{u\_0}}(\widehat{z}|\mathbf{u\_0}), ~~~~(1)
\end{align}


as the loss function which we want to minimize over the possible $k\in\mathcal{K}$.
Indeed, eq. (1) represents the measure of the agreement between our target detector $q\_{\widehat{Z}|\mathbf{u\_0}}$ and one of the possible $q^{(k)}_{\widehat{Z}|\mathbf{u\_0}}$.

Clearly if we knew which $\bar{k}\in\mathcal{K}$ created $\mathbf{x\_0}$ then the detector to pick would be  $\text{argmin}\_{q\_{\widehat{Z}|\mathbf{u\_0}}} \mathbb{E}\_{\widehat{Z}\sim q^{(\bar{k})}\_{\widehat{Z}|\mathbf{u\_0}}}\left[-\log q\_{\widehat{Z}|\mathbf{u\_0}}\right]$. Now, since we do not know $\bar{k}$, we need to account for all the possible $k\in\mathcal{K}$. If we have

\begin{align*}
\max\_{k\in\mathcal{K}} \mathbb{E}\_{\widehat{Z}\sim q^{(k)}\_{\widehat{Z}|\mathbf{u\_0}}}\left[-\log q\_{\widehat{Z}|\mathbf{u\_0}}\right]
\end{align*}

we can upper bound the error of $q\_{\widehat{Z}|\mathbf{u\_0}}$ for the other cases, i.e.

\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}\_{\widehat{Z}\sim q^{(k)}\_{\widehat{Z}|\mathbf{u\_0}}}\left[-\log q\_{\widehat{Z}|\mathbf{u\_0}}\right] \leq \max\_{k\in\mathcal{K}}\mathbb{E}\_{\widehat{Z}\sim q^{(k)}\_{\widehat{Z}|\mathbf{u\_0}}}\left[-\log q\_{\widehat{Z}|\mathbf{u\_0}}\right],
\end{align*}

hence

\begin{align*}
q^\star\_{\widehat{Z}|\mathbf{u\_0}} = \text{argmin}\_{q\_{\widehat{Z}|\mathbf{u\_0}}}\max\_{k\in\mathcal{K}}\mathbb{E}\_{\widehat{Z}\sim q^{(k)}\_{\widehat{Z}|\mathbf{u\_0}}}\left[-\log q\_{\widehat{Z}|\mathbf{u\_0}}\right].
\end{align*}
